 - **[Deployment / Distribution] `cargo install --path .`, with the plugin compiled into the binary (KEYSTONE).** `hooks/hooks.json`, `.mcp.json` and `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` are embedded via `include_str!`, and `plugin.json`'s version comes from `CARGO_PKG_VERSION`. At start, `viola run` copies its own exe to `<viola home>/bin/<CARGO_PKG_VERSION>-<short content hash>/viola(.exe)` if that copy is absent, and re-hashes an existing copy before reusing it: a copy that no longer matches refuses the start (exit 1, `pinned-hash-mismatch`) and is left as found. On Windows x64 the binary also embeds Microsoft's ConPTY companions (`src/conpty.rs`: `include_bytes!` of `vendor/conpty/<version>/x64/{OpenConsole.exe,conpty.dll}`; the package version, the nupkg SHA-256 and the two file SHA-256s have that file's text as their one home) and writes them write-if-absent to `bin/<version>-<hash>/conpty/` (`viola_state::pin::pin_companions`; never overwritten). At every start each companion is opened with `FILE_SHARE_READ` only and re-hashed in full against its pin, and the handles are held until the spawn. Unlike the exe, a mismatched companion never refuses the start: it is left as found, never loaded, and the child runs on the inbox ConPTY ([PTY]). The subdirectory, not the pinned dir itself, because the pinned dir is first on the child's PATH. The first start of a version writes the two files (1 176 216 B); as measured at chunk 2026-09-29-sideloaded-conpty on the dev host under a parallel test suite, `pin_companions` took a median 1 193 ms there (each write ~0.55 s), while CI's perf and test jobs stayed green (ci#36566391084) — the root tests seed their homes with the companions instead (test-plan §5), and no bound was raised. It rewrites the plugin files on every start to `<viola home>/plugin/<CARGO_PKG_VERSION>-<short content hash>/` and substitutes the copy's path (forward slashes) into every exec-form `command` in `hooks.json` and `.mcp.json`. It then passes that folder with `--plugin-dir`, sets `VIOLA_BIN` to the copy and puts the copy's folder first on the child's PATH. `hooks.json` registers seven exec-form entries, each `"command": "@@VIOLA_BIN@@"` (substituted at start) with `"args": ["hook", "<kebab event>"]`: SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit and Stop with `"timeout": 5`; SessionEnd with no `timeout` key; Notification, PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure with `"async": true`. The dialog tier joins them (chunk 2026-10-04-dialog-answers-by-dialog-id): PreToolUse with `"matcher": "AskUserQuestion|ExitPlanMode"` and PermissionRequest, each with `"timeout": 3615` — nine exec-form entries in all. `.mcp.json` registers the server `viola` as `{"mcpServers": {"viola": {"command": "@@VIOLA_BIN@@", "args": ["mcp"]}}}`, the pinned copy's absolute path substituted at every start (chunk 2026-10-17-mcp-server-for-drivers; until the `mcp` verb existed it shipped `{"mcpServers": {}}`, on the operator ruling at the 2026-09-27-instance-state-and-start-order plan that a registered command that exits 2 would block Claude Code). A registered `viola mcp` that exits 1 (its home refused by `home_passes`) does not block the CLI's start: the session reaches `idle` and the CLI logs that server's connection as failed (as measured on claude 2.1.287 on the Linux dev host at `viola-0.1.0/chunks/2026-10-18-plugin-precedence-live-readings/evidence/live-round.md`; its Windows leg owed to "Windows-only live measurements"). Hooks and MCP never search PATH. Rationale: the binary and the plugin can no longer drift apart. The prototype proved that PATH-found hooks run a different binary from the wrapper after a rebuild. The content hash is part of the key because `CARGO_PKG_VERSION` stays `0.1.0` across development rebuilds, so the version alone would collide. A rebuild therefore never changes what a running session calls. Known trade-off: a hook fix applies only to sessions started after the rebuild. The per-frame `v` + sender version stays as the second safety net, for the optional user-level install (`viola plugin install`), which is not bound to a wrapper's pinned copy.
