esponse` lines, one client-side `send-refused` per refused `send` text, the `list` tool's `agents-probe` pair and the board's `state-recovered` / `parse-rejected` lines (the `config-json` rejections included), and `process-exit` on stdin EOF — none when Claude Code ends it by a signal, as measured live on claude 2.1.287 at that chunk; `hook-<name>.ndjson`: `hook-invoked`, `hook-decision`, `parse-rejected`; `hook`'s drift reports go only to the instance's `diagnostics/detail-hook.ndjson`). One line per `write`; dir 0700, files 0600 on Unix; line format owned by obs and fixed by `schemas/diag-line.v1.json`. Content-bearing detail stays in `instances/<ViolaName>/diagnostics/`.
-- `instances/<ViolaName>/events.ndjson`, `events.ndjson.lock`, `snapshot.json`, `snapshot.json.lock`, `heartbeat`, `settings.json` (the per-session settings override that wraps the statusline; Unix only, see Claude Code integration names), `diagnostics/` (the owner-only content-bearing detail files `detail-<role>.ndjson`, such as the panic payload and backtrace in `detail-run.ndjson`; dir 0700, files 0600 on Unix; one `write` per line; file format owned by obs and fixed by `schemas/diag-detail.v1.json`. Codes-only process lines go to the home-level role files). v1 never rotates or truncates `events.ndjson`, because `wait`'s `after` and `send`'s `cursor` are byte offsets into it. Bounding its size is an open item, and any scheme must keep existing offsets valid. Three readers check these files before they use them. `viola list` (chunk 2026-10-11-the-board-viola-list): `viola_state::board::scan` runs `check_path` on the home and on `instances/`, each that exists (absent passes, an unstattable one is refused), reads each entry's type without following a link (a link, a non-directory or a name that is no `ViolaName` gives no instance and is only counted), joins a path only from a parsed name, then runs `check_instance` per instance in name order, all before any file under the home is opened and before its log starts; the first failure refuses the whole board with exit 21 (`--json`: `{"v":1,"error":"instance-unreachable","detail":null}` on stdout, stderr empty; human: the instance-state or the home-state `unable:` / `hint:` pair on stderr, stdout empty), no log opened, no line written and the home's mode left as found — the founder's own answer, 2026-10-13, relayed verbatim by the Viola overseer (the operator's answer at that chunk's planning, superseded 2026-10-13). `viola revive` (the start arm and `--list`) runs `viola_state::strict::check_instance` over the home, `instances/`, the instance directory, its `snapshot.json` and its `events.ndjson`, each that exists and in that order, before its wrapper log opens, and a refusal is exit 1 with the state-files `unable:` / `hint:` pair (under `--json` the `{"v":1,"error":"strict-modes-failed","detail":null}` document on stdout, stderr empty): no log opened, no line written (no `process-start`, no `process-exit`), no child started and the home's mode left as found. `viola hook statusline` runs the same check on its canonicalised instance directory before anything under the home is read or opened; a refused instance returns exit 0 at once with no `config.json` read, no role file opened, no `hook-invoked` or `hook-decision` line, no stdin read, no command run, nothing printed and `budget.json` untouched. Neither arm writes the word `strict-modes-failed` on any line. Both checks come first because opening a log sets an existing home to 0700 (as measured on the statusline arm at chunk 2026-10-10-statusline-pass-through, `evidence/red-green.md` §3), and a check made after it passes a home at 0770. Both orders were fixed at chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor: before it `revive`'s start arm opened its log first, and a home at 0770 came back 0700 and passed the check (as measured at that chunk's `research.md`, runs C and D, and `evidence/red-green.md` (a)), and the statusline arm opened its log after a refused check, so its refusal lasted one invocation (`evidence/red-green.md` (b)). That a refused check leaves no trace on the statusline arm is the founder's own answer, 2026-10-13, relayed verbatim by the Viola overseer (the operator's answer at that chunk's planning, superseded 2026-10-13); that it writes no line on `revive`'s start arm is the operator's answer at that chunk's planning, and the founder has not ruled on it. `check_instance` has these two product call sites (the rule: `check_instance(` under `src/`). The check is proven on Unix (five widened modes). No case widens the DACL of an instance tree on Windows yet; that case is owed to the working-route entry "Home and code-bearing file integrity".
